    "q-277850": {
      "dom_id": "step-q277850",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3{e}^{x}-{e}^{3/2}}{2\\sqrt{e}}>e.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}",
      "worked_solution": "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{e}^{x}-{e}^{3/2}}{2\\sqrt{e}} & >e \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-{e}^{3/2} & >2e\\sqrt{e} \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-{e}^{3/2} & >2e\\cdot {e}^{1/2} \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-{e}^{3/2} & >2{e}^{(1+\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\ 3{e}^{x}-{e}^{3/2} & >2{e}^{3/2} \\\\ 3{e}^{x} & >3{e}^{3/2} \\\\ {e}^{x} & >{e}^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x}) & >\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3/2}) \\\\ x & >\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
