    "q-365305": {
      "dom_id": "step-q365305",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Solve the inequality $3e-\\frac{1}{2}{(0.99)}^{x}>-2e.$",
      "worked_solution": "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3e-\\frac{1}{2}{(0.99)}^{x} & >-2e \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}{(0.99)}^{x} & >-5e \\\\ {(0.99)}^{x} & <10e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[(0.99)^{x}] & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10e) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.99) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10e)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.99)$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10e)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.99)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-365305-a",
              "type": "math",
              "value": "x>\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10e)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.98)}",
