    "q-87829": {
      "dom_id": "step-q87829",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Solve the inequality ${(0.45)}^{x+3}-10\\ge 0.$",
      "worked_solution": "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(0.45)}^{x+3}-10 & \\ge 0 \\\\ {(0.45)}^{x+3} & \\ge 10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[(0.45)^{x+3}] & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡(10) \\\\ (x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.45) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.45)$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.45)} \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.45)}-3\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
