    "q-115231": {
      "dom_id": "step-q115231",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3}{2}(0.43)^{4x+5}+4>-2.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}(0.43)^{4x+5}+4 & >-2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(0.43)^{4x+5} & >-6 \\\\ (0.43)^{4x+5} & >-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $0.43>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n(0.43)^{4x+5}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nIn particular, every value of $(0.43)^{4x+5}$ is greater than $-4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n(0.43)^{4x+5}>-4\n$$\n\nfor all real values of $x.$\n\nHence, the original inequality is satisfied for all real numbers.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
