    "q-365328": {
      "dom_id": "step-q365328",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "Solve the inequality $3\\cdot {2}^{1-3x}\\ge {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x-4}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {2}^{1-3x} & \\ge {(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x-4} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot {2}^{1-3x}) & \\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x-4}] \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(1-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\ge (x-4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(1-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\ge (x-4)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(1-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\ge (x-4)(0-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+(1-3x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\ge (4-x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\ge 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\ge 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & \\ge 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8.$ Since $3<8,$ we know that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3<\\operatorname{ln}⁡8.$ Therefore, $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡8<0.$ Because we are dividing both sides of the inequality by a negative number, we must reverse the direction of the inequality sign:\n\n$$\nx\\le \\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
