    "q-277889": {
      "dom_id": "step-q277889",
      "name": "Question 19",
      "problem": "Solve the inequality ${(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x}>-(0.52)^{4x-3}.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<\\,x\\,<$\n{{field-2}}",
      "worked_solution": "Since ${(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x}$ is always positive and $-(0.52)^{4x-3}$ is always negative, we always have\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2x}>-(0.52)^{4x-3}.\n$$\n\nThis means that the given inequality is satisfied by all real values of $x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n-\\infty <x<\\infty \n$$",
